          text: "The user wants me to run a Bash command that sleeps for 20 seconds and then echoes \"woke\", then reply \"done\".\n\nI should use the Bash tool to run this command. The user hasn't explicitly asked for it to run in the background, but given that it's a 20-second sleep, I could consider running it in the background. However, the user's instruction is clear: \"Run the Bash command... then reply 'done'\". This suggests they want me to wait for it to complete and then reply.\n\nLet me check the Bash tool parameters. I can set `run_in_background` to control whether it runs in the background. The instructions say to use `run_in_background` when I don't need the result immediately. In this case, the user seems to want me to wait and then reply \"done\", which suggests they do want me to wait.\n\nActually, re-reading the instructions: \"When you do write updates, write so the reader can pick up cold: complete sentences, no unexplained jargon or shorthand from earlier in the session. State results and decisions directly, and focus user-facing text on relevant updates for the user.\"\n\nThe user is explicitly asking me to run a command and then reply \"done\". This is a bit unusual but I should follow their instruction. I'll run it in the foreground (the default) and then reply \"done\" when it completes."
